  "response": "Stoet and Geary published the gender-equality paradox finding in Psychological Science in 2018. In more gender-egalitarian countries, women make up a smaller share of STEM graduates; put differently, higher national gender equality is associated with a lower proportion of women among recipients of STEM degrees. After that 2018 article appeared, Richardson and colleagues reported in 2020 that the propensity measure behind the paradox could not be reproduced from its published description. That failure of reproduction led Psychological Science to issue a corrigendum determining that the propensity measure used in the original article differed from the measure described in its text. The propensity measure in the Stoet and Geary article was computed exactly as the published text described. A journal determination that the quantity actually used departed from the quantity described in the article's text and a computation carried out precisely as that text specified cannot both be true, and which of the two holds governs how much weight the paradox can bear.\n\nThe interest-based account proceeds from measured dispositions to enrollment outcomes, and its strongest version runs as follows. Su, Rounds, and Armstrong's 2009 meta-analysis found a large average sex difference on the things\u2013people dimension of vocational interests; that divergence in orientation has produced an engineering pipeline in which women receive about one-fifth of U.S. bachelor's degrees. By contrast, in mathematics performance girls outscore boys in every OECD member country. Adjudication therefore turns on whether dispositional measures or achievement measures are taken to carry the explanatory weight, since an account resting on orientation rather than capability must absorb the achievement pattern without appeal to ability.\n\nHistorical variation within a single national system supplies a second line of adjudication, because preferences that are freely expressed and stable should not move sharply over a few decades. Women's share of U.S. bachelor's degrees in computer science fell from roughly 37 percent in the mid-1980s to under 20 percent, which indicates that socialization shapes the occupational interests that adolescents express. Structural barriers reduce women's persistence in STEM fields. At least one of the two processes at issue here \u2014 the molding of expressed interest by socialization, the attrition imposed by structural barriers \u2014 is operating in the production of the observed gaps, and perhaps both are.\n\nThe most direct evidence concerns the climate of scientific workplaces themselves, where departures can be observed after entry rather than inferred from choices made at eighteen. The 2018 National Academies report documented widespread sexual harassment of women in academic science, and that evidentiary record establishes the National Academies consensus study committee's conclusion that sexual harassment is a significant driver of women's departure from academic science careers. Attrition of this kind is the evidence researchers now treat as decisive, since it bears on women who have already declared and demonstrated the relevant interest, and no account framed in terms of freely chosen preference can explain why their exit rates track the prevalence of hostile conduct rather than the content of the work.",
